  "notes": "flood: the founder is founder_address of any version whose founder and slug derive the channel_id, or the address the reader came by. bound_keys are every inbox key the relay_records bind to each role holder, revoked or not, from the bind records and the keys that carried each verified revocation; reads lists every read the reader makes, one key each: the founder's keys under #t, then from each version walked, each key of the founder and of the admins that version names under #s. matches are the case's versions each filter returns; the junk matches none of the role holders' keys. s_walks are the versions walked from: none of the junk, since only those reads return versions to walk from. In the rotation case A2_by_M is signed by KM, which M revoked from KM itself after re-signing from KM2. That revocation has the pubkey, kind and d of bind_M_KM, so a relay that keeps the newest event per (pubkey, kind, d) holds it instead of bind_M_KM, as relay_records shows: KM is found as the key that carried the revocation. An earlier version stands on its binding, so the chain is G, A2_by_M, R3_by_M_KM2. Read with live keys only, or with keys from bind records only, the walk from G finds nothing and the reader would show G as current. Without the founder the reader has only the open read, which the junk fills to its bound, so it reports the channel as not read. walk_back: a reader that read A2_by_M but not the version it supersedes fetches that one by its exact d-tag. G and A3_close_by_F are signed by the same key; with one shared d-tag a relay that keeps the newest event per (pubkey, kind, d) would keep only A3, and the chain could not be walked back to G. With per-version d-tags it keeps both."
}
